/T (Understanding Inverse Scattering Problems \(ISP\) 1. Definition and Importance  Inverse Scattering Problems \(ISP\) are fundamental mathematical formulations aimed at  reconstructing unknown properties of a medium or object based on the interaction of waves  with it. This process involves deducing the size, shape, position, and material properties of the  object from the scattered wavefield measurements. ISPs are crucial in many fields, including  biomedical imaging, geophysics, nondestructive testing, and remote sensing. In the biomedical  domain, ISPs form the backbone of microwave imaging systems, enabling the detection and  characterization of anomalies such as tumors.  2. Principles of Scattering )
/E (Understanding Inverse Scattering Problems \(ISP\) 1. Definition and Importance  Inverse Scattering Problems \(ISP\) are fundamental mathematical formulations aimed at  reconstructing unknown properties of a medium or object based on the interaction of waves  with it. This process involves deducing the size, shape, position, and material properties of the  object from the scattered wavefield measurements. ISPs are crucial in many fields, including  biomedical imaging, geophysics, nondestructive testing, and remote sensing. In the biomedical  domain, ISPs form the backbone of microwave imaging systems, enabling the detection and  characterization of anomalies such as tumors.  2. Principles of Scattering )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
