/T (Forward Problem: Predicting the scattered field based on known medium properties and wave interactions. This step uses Maxwell's equations to model how electromagnetic waves propagate through the medium. The forward problem is generally well-posed, meaning it has a unique solution that depends continuously on the input. Inverse Problem: Reconstructing the medium's properties from the measured scattered field. This step is inherently ill-posed, as the solution may not be unique, and small errors in the input \(measurement noise\) can lead to large deviations in the output. )
/E (Forward Problem: Predicting the scattered field based on known medium properties and wave interactions. This step uses Maxwell's equations to model how electromagnetic waves propagate through the medium. The forward problem is generally well-posed, meaning it has a unique solution that depends continuously on the input. Inverse Problem: Reconstructing the medium's properties from the measured scattered field. This step is inherently ill-posed, as the solution may not be unique, and small errors in the input \(measurement noise\) can lead to large deviations in the output. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [11]
>>
